  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nEarly this morning, water vapor satellite imagery and upper air \nanalysis showed a broad upper level trough across the northern \nPlains, with the trough axis extending southward to the KS border. A\nbroad upper level ridge extended from southern CA, east into west \nTX. A cutoff low was located across the southeast US.\n\nThe 6Z surface map showed a cold front extending from eastern IA, \nsouthwest across northwest MO, southwest across east central KS (from\nwest of LWC, to west of EMP). The front then extended southwest into\nnorthwest OK, then west across the northern TX PNHDL. Behind the \nfront across the northern and western counties of the CWA, \ntemperatures were in the mid 60s. Ahead of the front across portions \nof east central KS, the temperatures were in the mid 70s.\n\nToday through Tonight:\n\nThe southern extent of the northern Plains H5 trough will shift \neast-southeast across KS this afternoon. Most CAMs show the surface \nfront across east central KS, shifting south of the CWA by this \nafternoon. A lead perturbation within the H5 trough will be lifting \nnortheast across IA. The only CAM that shows higher QPF is the Nam12.\nMost others only show isolated showers through the day north of the \nfront. Highs this afternoon will only reach the mid 70s across the \nnorthern counties of the CWA. Farther south, closer to the front, \nhighs will reach the lower to mid 80s across the southeast counties.\n\nTonight, the LLJ will increase from the southwest up to 30 KTS. The \nresulting isentropic lift will provide enough ascent for scattered \nto numerous elevated thunderstorms to develop, with the higher Pops \naround 60 percent across the east central KS. A few of the storms \ncould be strong given steep mid-level lapse rates, close to 1000 \nJ/KG of MUCAPE, and 30 KTS of effective shear. The primary hazard \nfrom any strong storms will be small hail and brief heavy rainfall.\n\nThursday through Friday:\n\nThe broad H5 trough will move east into the upper Midwest and mid MS \nRiver Valley on Thursday. The surface front will move southward and \nbecome stationary across northern OK. A mid-level perturbation will \ndig southeast from the northern High Plains across NE and KS, while \na southerly LLJ will increase to 30 KTS. The LLJ jet will cause \nisentropic lift, and combined with ascent ahead of the H5 trough \nwill cause showers and elevated thunderstorms to develop Thursday \nevening into early Friday morning. NBM PoPs are over 80s percent \nacross the central and east central counties of the CWA from 6Z-12Z \nFri.The LREF forecast over a 50 percent chance that east central KS \nwill receive 0.25\" or more of QPF Thursday night through Friday \nmorning. The surface front will lift north into NE by Friday evening.\nThe H5 ridge across the southwest US will build east across central \nHigh Plains into southern KS and northern OK. This should keep any \nmid-level perturbations digging southeast to the north and east of \nthe CWA, so the rain chances will drop during the afternoon hours of\nFriday.\n\nHighs on Thursday will reach the lower 80s north to mid and upper \n80s south, with more insolation during the afternoon hours.\n\nFriday night through Monday:\n\nThe extended range models are in good agreement with the southwest \nUS H5 ridge axis expanding east into the southern and central Plains\nby early next week. Highs will warm into the upper 80s to mid 90s on\nSaturday, with lower to mid 90s Sunday and Monday. Southerly surface\nwinds will advect richer moisture northward Sunday and Monday, thus \nafternoon dewpoints across east central KS may not mix out much, with\ndewpoints in the lower 70s. Therefore, afternoon heat indices may \nreach the 100-105 degree level across east central KS for Sunday and \nMonday afternoon.\n\nMonday night through Wednesday:\n\nThe GFS and Canadian models forecast the H5 ridge to retrograde \nwestward into NM/west TX. The ECMWF model forecast the H5 ridge to \nremain in the region with a slight shift southward of the center of \nthe H5 ridge axis into TX. If the GFS and Canadian models turn out \nto be correct, the mid level flow will become northwesterly and \nthere may be a chance for showers and thunderstorms ahead of any \nembedded perturbation in the northwest flow aloft. The ECMWF looks to\nbe drier and a bit hotter with high only retreating slightly into \nthe lower to mid 90s.",
